\begin{slide}
Filter Performance and Integrity Monitoring
 \begin{itemize}
 \item Covariance Monitoring
 \item Innovations Monitoring
 \item Corrective Action
\end{itemize}
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring
\begin{itemize}
  \item Ensuring Symmetry
  \item Managing Observability
  \item Ensuring Positive Definiteness
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Ensuring Symmetry
\begin{itemize}
  \item $P$ is supposed to be positive definite and symmetric
  \item Symmetry means that $P\trans = P$, i.e., for each element $p_{ij}$ of $P$ we must have $p_{ij}=p_{ji}$
  \item Roundoff error can cause loss of symmetry
  \item The best way to ensure symmetry is to work only with items on or (say) above the diagonal and simply define elements below he diagonal by fiat.
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Ensuring Symmetry
\begin{itemize}
  \item Both $P(+)$ and $P(-)$ need to be symmetrized
\begin{itemize}
  \item After extrapolation: symmetrize
  \item After update: symmetrize
\end{itemize}
\end{itemize} 
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Managing Observability
\begin{itemize}
  \item Observability means there is a linear path from the measurements to states
  \item Structural observability determined by $\Phi$ and $H$:
\end{itemize}
 If $H$ is $1\times n$, where $n$ is the size of the state vector, then the state $x_0$ is said to be \textbf{observable }from $n$ scalar measurements $\set{z_0,\ldots,z_{n-1}}$ if the block matrix
\[
\begin{bmatrix}
H\trans&\Phi\trans H\trans & \cdots & (\Phi\trans)^{n-1}H\trans
\end{bmatrix}
\]
is of rank $n$
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Managing Observability
\begin{itemize}
  \item If unobservable states are also unstable, estimation errors will be unstable and $P$ will show symptoms 
\begin{itemize}
  \item $\text{diag}(P)\rightarrow \infty$
  \item I.e., one or more diagonal elements of $P$ will grow without bound
\end{itemize}
  \item Not the same as divergence: "feature" of the design
\end{itemize}
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Managing Observability
\begin{itemize}
  \item Note also that the state estimation error variances (i.e., the diagonals of $P$) will naturally grow in the absence of measurements
  \item Going for a long time without incorporating a measurement is equivalent to lack of observability in the associated states
  \item Use covariance analysis simulation to detect non-observability
  \item The only real solution is to add new measurements
\end{itemize}
\end{slide}
\begin{slide}
Covariance Monitoring:  Ensuring Positive Definiteness
\begin{itemize}
  \item $P$ is supposed to be positive definite
  \item This means that $x'Px>0$ whenever $x\neq0$
  \item States modeled as random constants or other deterministic states can cause loss of positivity
  \item Add covariance bumping:  Add small amounts of process noise to diagonal elements of $Q$ for these states
\end{itemize}
\end{slide}
